"Predicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41274view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"lineno\":287}); Total lines: 1041\nL269: Across all nine student–benchmark combinations, $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ is positively correlated with post-training performance ($\\rho=0.10$–$0.94$). A larger contrast means that PI more strongly suppresses the student’s sampled tokens at judge-identified error steps than at comparable positions from the same task. Thus, PI formats that concentrate the distributional shift at critical mistakes tend to produce larger training gains.\nL270: This relationship is particularly strong for 4B-Instruct on AppWorld ($\\rho=0.94$) and 30B-Instruct on ALFWorld ($\\rho=0.92$). On $\\tau^{2}$, the contrast remains positively associated with performance for 4B-Thinking ($\\rho=0.70$), even though several trajectory formats have negative values. These small or negative contrasts and limited OPSD gains suggest poorly targeted supervision. This interpretation is consistent with cite124†Kaur et al.\nL271: [9] , who show that privileged distillation can penalize productive reconsideration tokens in thinking models: supervision may insufficiently suppress errors while disrupting useful behavior.\nL272: Takeaway. Effective PI concentrates token-level suppression on actual mistakes rather than comparable non-error steps, yielding a larger positive $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$.\nL273: ## 4 How Well Does OPSD Combine with GRPO?\nL274: \nL275: Figure 4: OPSD+GRPO performance on ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$-retail. The top row shows performance across objective weights, and the bottom row shows training curves at $\\lambda=0.9$. Here, $\\lambda=0$ is vanilla OPSD and $\\lambda=1$ is GRPO; dashed lines show the matched GRPO baseline.\nL276: ### 4.1 Combined Training Objective\nL277: \nL278: Rather than optimizing over the many possible ways to combine token-level distillation and GRPO outcome rewards [cite125†21 ], we use a simple weighted objective to test whether PI identified as effective under vanilla OPSD remains effective when combined with GRPO. We use Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 for all experiments in this section. We combine the GRPO and OPSD signals at the advantage level:\nL279:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{OPSD+GRPO}}=\\lambda A^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}+(1-\\lambda)A^{\\mathrm{OPSD}},$$  |  | (7)\nL280: \nL281: where $A^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}$ is the outcome-based group-relative advantage, $A^{\\mathrm{OPSD}}$ is the token-level self-distillation advantage, and $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ controls their relative weight.\nL282: \nL283: Figure cite126†4 compares four values of $\\lambda$ across three PI formats with matched GRPO-only controls on ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$. The $\\lambda=0$ endpoint corresponds to vanilla OPSD.\nL284: ### 4.2 Effect of Objective Weighting\nL285: ##### PI quality persists: better PI is still better after adding GRPO.\nL286: \nL287: Trajectory PI performs best under vanilla OPSD and remains strongest across most values of $\\lambda$, followed by Skill and Checklist PI. This ordering is consistent through $\\lambda=0.99$ on all benchmarks. Thus, combining OPSD with outcome-based rewards does not eliminate differences in PI quality: stronger PI continues to provide a more effective auxiliary training signal.\nL288: Takeaway. The PI format that performs best under vanilla OPSD generally remains the strongest after adding GRPO, so outcome rewards complement rather than replace effective PI selection.\nL289: ##### GRPO strengthens OPSD.\nL290: Increasing $\\lambda$ generally improves performance for all three PI formats, with the largest gains occurring for the weaker Checklist and Skill formats on ALFWorld and AppWorld. On ALFWorld, several Skill and Trajectory configurations exceed the GRPO-only baseline score, with Trajectory PI reaching $95.13$ at $\\lambda=0.99$ versus the baseline score of $84.96$. On AppWorld, Trajectory PI at $\\lambda=0.99$ reaches $43.90$, exceeding the GRPO score of $42.91$.\nL291: Likewise, on $\\tau^{2}$, it reaches $55.04$, compared with $48.27$ for GRPO.\nL292: Takeaway. Increasing the GRPO weight substantially improves vanilla OPSD. With strong PI and appropriate weighting, the combined objective can outperform a matched GRPO baseline.\nL293: ##### PI improves sample efficiency, but its benefit fades once the policy has learned from it.\nL294: The training curves in Figure cite126†4 show that PI-guided training, particularly with Trajectory PI, improves rapidly during the initial steps. On ALFWorld, all three PI-guided curves plateau or decline after step 100, corresponding to one epoch, while GRPO continues to improve. This suggests that PI provides its largest optimization benefit early in training, before the policy has learned the information it can readily extract from PI.\nL295: Because each training task uses the same static PI throughout training, its supervision may become redundant after the first epoch. Beyond this point, the OPSD signal can become a distraction that interferes with further outcome-based learning. On AppWorld, Trajectory PI reaches nearly $100\\%$ training success at $\\lambda=0.9$ but only $39.14$ test pass@1, suggesting that this improved sample efficiency can also accelerate overfitting on small, less diverse training sets.\nL296: Takeaway. PI accelerates early learning, especially when it contains a strong reference trajectory. Its marginal benefit decreases after the policy has learned the most readily transferable behavior, after which continued OPSD can even hinder further improvement.\nL297: ## 5 Which Offline Metric Should Guide PI Choice?\nL298: We complement the observational analysis of Section cite23†3.2 with two controlled evaluations of the offline PI metrics. ALFWorld-Passcode isolates the effect of leaked information (Section cite34†5.1 ), and per-task selection evaluates whether the KL metrics can identify effective PI from candidate pools (Section cite38†5.2 ); Appendix cite68†E reports additional ablations with corrupted and length-matched PI.\nL299: Together, these studies further demonstrate the limitations of utility and how error-step KL contrast can guide PI selection in practice. Finally, we provide practical recommendations for using the proposed KL metrics. All experiments train Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 with vanilla OPSD on ALFWorld and report held-out pass@1, following Section cite16†2.3 .\nL300: ### 5.1 ALFWorld-Passcode: Leaked Information Can Distort PI Utility\nL301: ##### ALFWorld-Passcode.\nL302: We modify ALFWorld so that completing the task no longer ends the episode. Instead, the final observation reveals a task-specific key, and the episode is scored only on the key the agent submits. The verifier therefore checks only the submitted key, not whether the task was completed.\nL303: We build four PIs from the same GLM-5.2 reference trajectories (Action $+$ Obs.): Key only contains just the reveal and the submission of the key; Mismatched trajectory $+$ key precedes this task’s key with the trajectory of a different task of the same type; Trajectory $+$ key uses the task’s own trajectory, ending with the reveal and the submission of its key; and Trajectory $+$ decoy key is the same trajectory with a wrong key in the final submission.\nL304: Utility is measured as in Equation cite127†2 , with the PI in the student’s prompt. Figure cite128†5 reports OPSD performance, utility, and both KL metrics for each PI.\nL305: Figure 5: Each row is one PI built from the same GLM-5.2 trajectories on ALFWorld-Passcode. Utility ranks the PIs by whether they contain the correct key. $D$ and $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ rank them by whether they contain the task’s own trajectory, and so does OPSD performance. Utility is the untrained student’s pass@1 with the PI in its prompt, split into completed tasks (solid) and copied keys submitted without completing the task (hatched).\nL306: Dashed boxes show the same trajectory in standard ALFWorld, and dashed lines mark the student without PI.\nL307: ##### A PI can score near-perfect utility while teaching nothing.\nL308: With only the correct key in its prompt, the student reaches $98.1$ pass@1, yet training on this PI leaves it exactly where it started. Utility credits the PI for helping a policy that sees it; OPSD, however, trains a student that never does. Any benefit that depends on reading the PI, such as copying a key, therefore cannot transfer. Both KL metrics recognize this, assigning Key only the lowest $D$ and $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ among the four PIs.\nL309: ##### The key changes utility; the trajectory changes learning.\nL310: The remaining PIs vary the key and the trajectory one at a time. Changing only the key, from correct to decoy, moves utility by 25 points but leaves training performance, $D$, and $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ essentially unchanged. Changing only the trajectory, from the task’s own to another task’s, does the reverse.\nL311: The mismatched PI still scores higher utility than the task’s own trajectory in standard ALFWorld or with the decoy key, yet it nearly halves training performance to $45.0$ and lowers both KL metrics. Utility responds to the part of the PI the student can only copy, and the KL metrics to the part it can learn from.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41274view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":210}); Total lines: 1284\nL196: We ran all three on SPOT, Spiffy and the BlockSpec-style tree at $T\\in\\{0.5,1,1.5,2,2.5\\}$ (Table cite145†1 , Appendix cite88†F.6 ).\nL197: Table 1: Temperature sweep on SDAR-8B MBPP. Draft-free columns: target accuracy, argmax fraction, mean committed-token probability. SPOT $D8$: NFE and throughput against the $T$-matched draft-free control. Greedy-match columns show distribution drift. The $D8$ NFE standard deviation over seeds grows from $0.3$ at $T{=}0.5$ to $8.5$ at $T{=}2.5$. ^{†}Greedy match is not distribution-preserving, so its NFE is not comparable.\nL198:  | draft-free ($D0$)  | SPOT $D8$ NFE (tok/s ratio)  | greedy SPOT  | Spiffy $D8$ NFE\nL199: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL200: $T$  | acc  | argmax  | mean $p$  | coupled  | uncoupled  | greedy^{†}  | acc  | argmax  | coupled  | uncoupled\nL201: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL202: 0.5  | 0.35  | 0.986  | 0.993  | 58.2 (2.56)  | 58.2 (2.57)  | 57.6 (2.57)  | 0.36  | 0.978  | 84.3  | 84.0\nL203: 1.0  | 0.34  | 0.976  | 0.965  | 60.1 (2.45)  | 60.2 (2.43)  | 56.8 (2.55)  | 0.38  | 0.973  | 86.3  | 86.6\nL204: 1.5  | 0.08  | 0.866  | 0.828  | 74.0 (1.97)  | 82.6 (1.77)  | 57.4 (2.55)  | 0.28  | 0.958  | 94.2  | 108.9\nL205: 2.0  | 0.00  | 0.057  | 0.052  | 187.4 (0.78)  | 256.7 (0.54)  | 94.7 (1.52)  | 0.01  | 0.750  | 114.6  | 258.5\nL206: 2.5  | 0.00  | 0.001  | 0.000  | 200.8 (0.71)  | 269.1 (0.54)  | 128.1 (1.13)  | 0.00  | 0.582  | 110.5  | 269.2\nL207: (i) Coupled and uncoupled match are exact; greedy match is not. In both exact regimes the emitted tokens track the draft-free statistics within sampling noise: over all three drafters at $D8$ and $D20$ and $T\\leq 1.5$ (Table cite146†21 ), the emitted argmax fraction stays within $0.016$ of the draft-free value, the mean committed-token probability within $0.015$, and accuracy within $0.04$.\nL208: Greedy match drifts, and the drift grows with $T$: at $T{=}1.5$ it emits accuracy $0.28$ where the model samples $0.08$ and picks the argmax at $95.8\\%$ of commits against the model’s $86.6\\%$; the drift is proportional to acceptance, so it makes the best drafter the most biased. (ii) Coupling keeps acceptance alive as the target’s samples move away from its argmax.\nL209: At $T\\leq 1$ the sampled token is the argmax $98$–$99\\%$ of the time and the two verifiers agree within $1\\%$ in NFE; at $T{=}1.5$ uncoupled costs $12$–$16\\%$ more NFE for SPOT and Spiffy ($74.0\\to 82.6$ and $94.2\\to 108.9$); at $T{=}2$ uncoupled accepts almost nothing ($256.7$ NFE against $269.2$ draft-free) while coupled still drafts. The mechanism is the coupling bound of cite141†Daliri et al.\nL210: (2025) : a stale-informed proposal survives with probability at least $(1-\\delta)/(1+\\delta)$ in the total variation $\\delta$ between stale and fresh conditionals, whereas an independent sample equals the argmax only with probability $p_{\\max}$. (iii) At high temperatures, target quality becomes the limiting factor. Draft-free accuracy already drops sharply to $0.08$ at $T{=}1.5$ and reaches $0.00$ at $T\\geq 2$, leaving little useful generation quality to preserve.\nL211: ## 4 Method\nL212: ### 4.1 Setup\nL213: Let $p_{\\theta}$ be a block-causal diffusion language model (cite119†Cheng et al., 2026 ; cite147†Bie et al., 2025 ). Within an active block of length $L$, a state $x\\in\\mathcal{X}=(\\mathcal{V}\\cup\\{\\textsc{m}\\})^{L}$ assigns each position either a token in vocabulary $\\mathcal{V}$ or the mask symbol m, and $\\mathcal{M}(x)=\\{s:x_{s}=\\textsc{m}\\}$ denotes its masked positions. Given the completed causal context $C$, one target forward returns $p_{\\theta,s}(\\cdot\\mid x,C)$ for every $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(x)$.\nL214: Let $s^{\\star}(x)=\\argmax_{s\\in\\mathcal{M}(x)}\\max_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)$ be the highest-confidence masked position and $x\\oplus(s,v)$ be the state obtained by filling position $s$ with token $v$. At $T=0$, the target commits $v^{\\star}_{0}(x)=\\argmax_{v}p_{\\theta,s^{\\star}(x)}(v\\mid x,C)$, giving the deterministic transition\nL215:  | $$\\Phi_{0}(x)=x\\oplus\\bigl(s^{\\star}(x),v^{\\star}_{0}(x)\\bigr).$$  |  | (1)\nL216: \nL217: At $T>0$, the committed token is instead sampled from the temperature-adjusted conditional\nL218: \nL219:  | $$\\pi_{T,s}(v\\mid x,C)=\\frac{p_{\\theta,s}(v\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}{\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{\\theta,s}(u\\mid x,C)^{1/T}}.$$  |  | (2)\nL220: A drafter $\\mathcal{D}$ proposes a finite tree of hypothetical future states rooted at the current state, with each edge representing one slot–token commitment. The target evaluates the root and all drafted states in one batched forward. The verifier accepts an edge $(y,z)$ only when $z$ equals the target’s own next state from $y$, which we call state-equality verification; because the target has a unique next commitment, the accepted subset of a draft tree is always a single path.\nL221: Let $X_{T}^{\\mathrm{DF}}$ and $X_{T,\\mathcal{D}}^{\\mathrm{Spec}}$ denote the draft-free and speculative committed trajectories. We call a verifier distribution preserving when $X_{T,\\mathcal{D}}^{\\mathrm{Spec}}\\overset{d}{=}X_{T}^{\\mathrm{DF}}.$\nL222: ### 4.2 The drafter: learned commit order\nL223: #### Order model.\nL224: At speculative round $t$, let $\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(\\cdot)$ denote the most recently available target conditional at masked position $s$. These conditionals are stale: hypothetical future commitments change the true target distribution, but no additional target forward is performed while drafting. Our drafter is a three-layer transformer encoder ($d_{\\mathrm{model}}=128$, $8$ heads, $0.62$M parameters).\nL225: Each masked position is represented using its stale top-$32$ probabilities (raw and log), eight summary statistics, and two path features encoding the positions already committed along the hypothetical prefix and its depth. Given a hypothetical state $y$, the model predicts $q_{\\phi}(s\\mid y)$, where $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(y),$ a distribution over the target’s next committed position. The same prefix-conditional predictor is learned in Algorithm cite148†1 and recursively queried during Algorithm cite149†2 .\nL226: The drafter uses no target hidden states, token embeddings, or text, and can therefore be easily evaluated across different DLMs.\nL227: #### Training.\nL228: We train $q_{\\phi}$ entirely from draft-free target trajectories (Algorithm cite148†1 ). Let $(s_{t}^{\\star},s_{t+1}^{\\star},\\ldots)$ denote the target commit sequence following decoding state $x_{t}$. For each prefix depth $d$, the first $d$ commitments define a hypothetical state $y_{t,d}$ while the stale conditionals $\\widehat{p}_{t}$ remain fixed; the next target commitment $s_{t+d}^{\\star}$ is used as supervision.\nL229: This produces prefix-conditioned examples $(\\widehat{p}_{t},y_{t,d},s_{t+d}^{\\star})$ that match the states encountered during recursive drafting. We optimize $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{order}}(\\phi)=-\\mathbb{E}_{(\\widehat{p},y,s^{\\star})\\sim\\mathcal{E}}\\log q_{\\phi}(s^{\\star}\\mid y),$ where $\\mathcal{E}$ is the resulting training set. Full data-generation and optimization details are given in Appendix cite68†D.1 .\nL230: #### Inference.\nL231: At inference (Algorithm cite149†2 ), drafting starts from the current target state $x_{t}$ and stale conditionals $\\widehat{p}_{t}$. For every candidate prefix $y$, the order model scores the remaining positions using $q_{\\phi}(\\cdot\\mid y)$, and each position is paired with token hypotheses from the stale target conditional. Let $\\mathcal{H}_{t}(s)$ denote the token-hypothesis set for position $s$.\nL232: At $T=0$, its primary hypothesis is $\\widetilde{v}_{0}(s)=\\argmax_{v}\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(v),$ with the runner-up additionally included when the stale top-$1$ probability is below $0.6$. At $T>0$, $\\mathcal{H}_{t}(s)$ uses the coupled stale draw of Eq. cite150†4 , introduced formally in Section cite21†4.3 .\nL233: For a candidate child $z=y\\oplus(s,v)$, define its prefix mass recursively as\nL234: \nL235:  | $$m(z)=m(y)\\,q_{\\phi}(s\\mid y)\\,\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(v),\\qquad m(x_{t})=1.$$  |  | (3)\nL236: Algorithm cite149†2 uses DDTree, a level-batched best-first search adapted from cite139†Ringel and Romano (2026) , to allocate budget $D$ to the highest-mass candidate states. Since every factor in Eq. cite151†3 lies in $[0,1]$, a child’s mass cannot exceed its parent’s, allowing selected nodes to be expanded level-by-level while recovering the same top-$D$ fixpoint as sequential best-first search. A calibration temperature $\\tau$ is applied to $q_{\\phi}$ before search; the final model uses $\\tau=1$.\nL237: #### Adaptive budget and padding.\nL238: Algorithm cite149†2 treats $D$ as a hard upper bound. We additionally apply a prefix-mass floor $\\gamma$, discarding candidates with $m(z)<\\gamma$ and thereby adapting the realized verification budget to drafter confidence. Independently, the target batch may be padded with dummy replicas to maintain a fixed $(1+D)L$ shape and reduce batch-shape-dependent numerical variation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41274view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":295}); Total lines: 1284\nL284: Spiffy graph  | 94.3 (2.03)  | 75.5 (1.20)  | 86.3 (1.77)  | 64.8 (1.07)  | 89.4 (2.16)  | 68.9 (1.31)  | 103.5 (1.72)  | 87.7 (0.93)\nL285: free chain, RS  | 90.0 (1.54)  | 84.4 (0.85)  | 64.5 (1.86)  | 52.4 (1.12)  | 78.0 (1.84)  | 70.4 (1.05)  | 116.9 (1.14)  | 115.8 (0.60)\nL286: BlockSpec tree  | 139.3 (1.27)  | 118.2 (0.68)  | 138.3 (1.07)  | 115.2 (0.57)  | 138.4 (1.34)  | 115.5 (0.72)  | 140.7 (1.28)  | 121.0 (0.68)\nL287: In the upper block of Table cite144†2 , SPOT delivers $3.2$–$4.7\\times$ fewer target forwards than draft-free decoding at $D8$ and $4.1$–$6.8\\times$ at $D20$, at $2.0$–$2.7\\times$ single-device wall at $D8$; against Spiffy’s calibrated graph it has $15$–$35\\%$ fewer NFE and $1.1$–$1.5\\times$ its throughput in every task-and-budget setting, with larger margins against the chain and the tree.\nL288: The baselines’ throughput collapses with budget where their NFE does not: the tree is slower than draft-free at $D20$ on every task and the chain on three of four, since a wider draft costs $(1{+}D)L$ positions whether or not it is accepted, while they accept only $1.1$–$4.0$ tokens per round against SPOT’s $4.5$–$7.7$ (Appendix cite93†F.8 ).\nL289: SPOT-$\\gamma$ removes the budget dependence: its throughput ratio is flat in the budget ($2.3$–$3.2\\times$ at $D8$, $D12$ and $D20$ alike) and at or above the padded recipe in every SDAR-8B setting at both temperatures. The floor pays $4$–$24$ NFE per setting, so SPOT stays NFE-optimal while SPOT-$\\gamma$ still has fewer forwards than every baseline in $21$ of $24$ settings.\nL290: MRP (cite123†Xu et al., 2026 ), the closest learned drafter, reports $1.17$–$1.40\\times$ over one-token-per-step decoding on these same targets in a different implementation, against SPOT’s $2.0$–$3.6\\times$ at $D8$, a margin wider than that difference can account for, and its argmax-equality acceptance has no distribution-preserving extension to sampling (Table cite159†5 ).\nL291: ### 5.3 Sampling temperature: coupled-noise match against the baselines\nL292: The lower block of Table cite144†2 shows that nothing changes when the target samples at $T{=}1$: under the same verifier, SPOT keeps $15$–$34\\%$ fewer NFE than Spiffy and $1.15$–$1.48\\times$ its throughput. Free chain under rejection sampling is the strongest baseline on MBPP NFE, but stays $0.47$–$0.92$ below SPOT in throughput, since every accepted link pays an acceptance test, and collapses on MATH500 ($116$ NFE, $0.60\\times$ at $D20$).\nL293: Every $T{=}1$ result is distribution-preserving, with accuracies within $0.06$ of the control over seeds; tree $+$ coupled-noise match is the deployment configuration at any temperature (Appendix cite92†F.7 ). The ratios grow with generation length: at $512$ SPOT reaches $2.79\\times$ draft-free at $D8$ and SPOT-$\\gamma$ $3.23\\times$, against $2.45\\times$ and $2.86\\times$ at $256$ (Appendix cite87†F.5 ).\nL294: ### 5.4 Transferability within and across model families\nL295: Table 3: Checkpoint transfer ($T{=}0$, b32, $D8$, padded): each checkpoint pointed at a target with no retraining or recalibration, NFE per sample, the better drafter in bold. Both drafters run the same target weights over the same $(1{+}D)L$ positions per forward, so throughput tracks NFE (ratios in Table cite160†14 ). ^{†}approximate prefix cache of cite122†Wu et al. (2026) , not distribution-preserving.\nL296: NFE$\\downarrow$ at $D8$  | GSM8K  | MBPP  | HumanEval  | MATH500\nL297: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL298: target $\\backslash$ drafter  | SDAR-8B  | mini  | SDAR-8B  | mini  | SDAR-8B  | mini  | SDAR-8B  | mini\nL299: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL300: SDAR-8B  | 69.6  | 72.8  | 56.7  | 58.6  | 62.2  | 66.8  | 84.5  | 87.9\nL301: LLaDA2.0-mini  | 85.9  | 83.0  | 105.2  | 94.9  | 97.8  | 88.2  | 87.1  | 84.8\nL302: LLaDA-8B^{†}  | 79.8  | 83.1  | 54.5  | 56.7  | 83.3  | 87.6  | 83.6  | 86.3\nL303: Dream-7B^{†}  | 66.0  | 67.8  | 51.2  | 52.0  | 57.3  | 58.3  | 77.2  | 79.1\nL304: Transfer takes two forms. Re-running identical training recipe on SDAR-4B reproduces the SDAR-8B picture, lowest NFE and highest throughput in every comparison at $T{=}0$ and $T{=}1$, and on LLaDA2.0-mini, a mixture-of-experts target with a different tokenizer, it gives the lowest NFE across $36$ comparisons and the higher throughput in $34$.\nL305: On SDAR-1.7B SPOT keeps the lowest NFE everywhere but cedes three throughput columns at $T{=}0$ and two at $T{=}1$ to Spiffy, where a cheap target forward makes the padded draft much of the step. The method carries to block width 16 untuned (Appendices cite83†F.3 and cite86†F.4 ).\nL306: Table cite161†3 swaps the trained checkpoints between targets, tokenizers included, with no retraining or recalibration, and Table cite160†14 gives the full six-target, four-drafter matrix: the transferred drafter costs $3$–$7\\%$ NFE on SDAR-8B and still beats every baseline calibrated on SDAR-8B (Table cite144†2 ), and $3$–$11\\%$ on mini, where it stays ahead of every baseline on GSM8K and MATH500.\nL307: Beyond the block-causal family, LLaDA-8B (cite117†Nie et al., 2025 ) and Dream-7B (cite120†Ye et al., 2025 ) attend over the sequence, so drafted runs and control both use the approximate cache of cite122†Wu et al. (2026) (Appendix cite67†D ) and these rows measure reach rather than losslessness: checkpoints that never saw either model cut LLaDA-8B’s $256$ forwards to $55$–$88$ and Dream-7B’s to $51$–$79$: the order model learns where a diffusion target commits, and much of that carries.\nL308: ### 5.5 Ablations\nL309: Table cite162†17 (Appendix cite88†F.6 ) varies one component on SDAR-8B MBPP. The largest effect comes from replacing the tree with a chain: NFE increases by $7.3$ at $D8$ and by $11.9$ at $D20$, while ratio drops from $1.72$ to $1.34$. Budget therefore pays through branching, not a longer chain. The token hedge and the scaled corpus are each worth under one NFE, and padding moves throughput by at most $0.04$.\nL310: The mass floor trades NFE for wall-clock speed, but only without padding: at $D20$, $\\gamma=0/0.05/0.1$ gives $39.6/44.3/47.8$ NFE at $1.76/2.84/2.99\\times$ wall, by computing fewer verification positions.\nL311: ## 6 Discussion\nL312: DLM decoding remains constrained by a fundamental speed–quality trade-off. SPOT addresses this challenge with a two-fold solution. We show that stale target distributions already contain highly reliable token information, making commit order the key missing signal for effective speculation. A lightweight $0.62$M-parameter model predicts this order and constructs multi-step draft trees, while coupled-noise verification preserves the target distribution under sampling.\nL313: SPOT recovers $54$–$81\\%$ of the cost-aware oracle ceiling, uses $15$–$35\\%$ fewer target forwards than an offline-calibrated draft graph at up to $1.5\\times$ its throughput, and transfers across six DLMs without retraining.\nL314: More broadly, our results show that substantially faster DLM generation can be achieved without sacrificing the quality or exactness of the underlying decoding process, establishing commit-order prediction as a promising foundation for the next generation of speculative decoding methods for DLMs.\nL315: #### Limitations.\nL316: \nL317: The main limitation of SPOT is that its gains diminish when verification is costly or acceptance is low. Verification becomes the bottleneck at wide blocks, while exact tree packing remains unresolved under bidirectional attention. Gains also narrow on low-acceptance tasks, and at $T\\geq 2$ the $T{=}0$-trained order model is outperformed by noise-aware ranking (Appendix cite88†F.6 ).\nL318: ## AI use statement\nL319: Generative AI tools were used during manuscript preparation to support language refinement and editing, and during development to assist with implementation of the experimental codebase. They were not used to formulate the research questions, develop the proposed method, define the evaluation procedure, or draw conclusions from the experimental findings. The quantitative results reported in this paper originate from the experiments described in Section cite26†5.1 and the appendix.\nL320: The authors inspected and verified all AI-assisted contributions, including code incorporated into the experimental workflow, and remain fully responsible for the manuscript, implementation, experimental results, and conclusions.\nL321: ### Ethics statement\nL322: \nL323: This work focuses on accelerating inference in diffusion large language models and does not involve human subjects or the collection of personal data. We do not anticipate any ethical concerns beyond those associated with the underlying language models and their downstream applications.\nL324: ### Reproducibility statement\nL325: \nL326: Code and the trained drafter checkpoints are available at cite163†https://github.com/Eliezer318/SPOT-dLLM†github.com , with instructions to reproduce the experiments. Every number in this paper comes from one benchmark run on one machine (eight identical NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada cards; one job per card), with the protocol of Section cite26†5.1 and Appendix cite67†D .\nL327: ## References\n"
